{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Table 2.} The six benchmark datasets. {\i split} separates within-dataset holdouts from cross-dataset and cross-study transfer; {\i genes} records whether the matrix is keyed by Ensembl ids or by gene symbols, which decides who can read it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The blood+gut set is a subsample of {\b IBDverse} (Wellcome Sanger Institute; blood, terminal ileum and rectum from 421 individuals), included here because 86 fine-grained labels make it the high-cardinality stress case for the panel. It carries no Cell Ontology ids, so it contributes to accuracy and macro-F1 but to no ontology-scored result.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The set spans the three generalization regimes (within-dataset, across datasets, across studies), an order of magnitude in cell-type count (8\u8594 ?86), and both gene-ID conventions.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Metrics\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Per (dataset, method, repeat): {\b accuracy}; {\b macro-F1}, unweighted over classes so rare types count as much as common ones; {\b ontology-aware concordance}; {\b fit time}, {\b predict time}, and {\b peak RSS} (resident set size \u8212- the maximum physical memory the process held).\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Ontology-aware concordance} credits a call that is the same node, an ancestor, or a descendant of the truth in the Cell Ontology. It exists because cell-type vocabularies disagree about granularity more than about biology: {\f1 periportal region hepatocyte} against {\f1 Hepatocyte} is a right-lineage-wrong-depth call, which exact match scores as a total failure and a working scientist would accept. It is the {\b only} metric that compares methods with different label vocabularies (\u167 ?2.2), and it is reported only where both reference and query carry CL ids \u8212- so not on {\f1 blood_gut_intra}, and not on the symbol-keyed {\f1 pbmc}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Concordance depends on the ontology release, since ancestor sets change between them. All numbers here use the {\b Cell Ontology basic release of 2026-06-08} (sha256 73996c63\u8230 ?), resolved once and reused by every scoring pass. This is not a hypothetical concern: re-running lung_cross against a later-fetched release reproduced the splits exactly (14,390 reference / 13,550 query cells) and still moved concordance by 0.003, with no model changing. That release, its checksum, and pinned versions for all six benchmark environments are recorded as lockfiles in the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://github.com/iandriver/actinn-jax-benchmark"}}{\fldrslt{\ul
benchmark repository
}}}
, which also carries a command that reports any drift from them.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Exact-match accuracy is reported for the ten reference-trained methods and, on {\f1 lung_cross}, flagged: reference and query use different label vocabularies there, so its exact accuracy is a vocabulary artifact and the ranking, not the level, is what transfers.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Harness, isolation, and hardware\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Each method runs in {\b its own virtual environment, as a separate process}, because the dependency sets are mutually unsatisfiable \u8212- scVI-based methods, TensorFlow/Keras (Pan-human Azimuth), R (SingleR, scmap) and the JAX core cannot coexist in one interpreter. One driver builds the reference/query split {\b once per dataset} from a fixed seed and hands the identical pair to every method, so no method sees a different split. {\b repeats = 3}; scPRINT and Pan-human Azimuth run once each, being deterministic given a fixed query and dominated by a single forward pass.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Resource accounting is per subprocess: fit and predict are timed separately and peak RSS is sampled by a monitor thread in the child, so one method\u8217's footprint cannot be attributed to another.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Hardware}: Apple Silicon (M-series), CPU for the classical, linear, correlation and pretrained tiers and for actinn-jax; Apple MPS for the deep and foundation tiers. No discrete GPU, no cloud \u8212- the regime the benchmark is about. Math-library threads are left uncapped, so wall-clock is what a practitioner sees; an optional thread cap exists for stricter reproducibility. \u167 ?3.7 repeats the comparison on one cloud CPU instance, where matched concurrency matters and is discussed (\u167 ?2.10).\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Building a broad reference\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The shipped broad-pass references (\u167 ?3.4) are built once, offline, by a three-stage pipeline driven by one command (S5 in the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://github.com/iandriver/actinn-jax-benchmark"}}{\fldrslt{\ul
benchmark repository
}}}
).\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Sampling.} Every primary cell for one organism in the CELLxGENE Census {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://chanzuckerberg.github.io/cellxgene-census/"}}{\fldrslt{\ul
[CZI Census 2025]
}}}
 \u8212- primary meaning the copy the census designates canonical, so cells deposited in more than one study are counted once \u8212- stratified by cell type at a fixed cap per type: 40 for {\f1 broad_human_v1}, 60 for the later human and mouse pulls. Types with fewer than 12 cells are dropped, as are labels that carry no information (\u8220"unknown\u8221", \u8220"native cell\u8221", \u8220"eukaryotic cell\u8221", \u8220"animal cell\u8221"). The pull is checkpointed every ten datasets and retried, since transient object-store read errors are routine at this scale. Genes are carried under both Ensembl ids and symbols, because a symbol-keyed consumer cannot use an Ensembl-only pull. The census \u8220"stable\u8221" alias is a moving pointer, so the release it resolved to is recorded; every reference here comes from {\b census 2025-11-08}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Coarse hierarchy \u8212- two interchangeable routes.} The model is a coarse classifier over groups plus one fine classifier per group, and the grouping comes from either:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 1.\tx360\tab {\b Foundation-model embeddings.} scPRINT (medium-v1.5) embeds the reference once in 4,000-cell chunks; per-cell-type centroids are Ward-clustered into roughly \u8730 ?(number of types) groups, with a floor of eight. scPRINT\u8217's QC drops cells, so the embedding is not positionally aligned to the input and the label is carried through the model with the vectors. This is the only step that wants a GPU/MPS.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 2.\tx360\tab {\b Cell Ontology lineage.} Each cell type is described by a binary indicator over the CL terms it descends from; terms appearing in fewer than two types, or in all of them, are dropped as uninformative; the same Ward clustering is applied to those vectors. Free, deterministic, and species-independent \u8212- which is what makes the pan-mouse reference possible, since the distillation teacher is human-only and scPRINT\u8217's mouse support is untested here. \u167 ?3.4 compares the two routes against a random-grouping control.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Training and calibration.} A 4,000-gene panel of {\b highly variable genes (HVGs)} \u8212- the genes that differ most across cells, and so carry most of the signal for telling types apart \u8212- is selected on the reference; the coarse and per-group fine models are trained on it. Abstain calibration holds out {\b 10% of cell types entirely} as out-of-distribution (OOD \u8212- types the model was never trained on, standing in for the novel populations a real query contains), plus a 20% within-type test split, and sweeps the confidence threshold to trade coverage against accuracy on kept cells \u8212- the table each shipped reference reports.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Shipping and verification.} Each reference ships with a build record: census release, corpus sizes, hierarchy route, organism and calibration table. Because a rebuild can train cleanly and annotate {\i worse}, the previous reference is backed up and the new one is scored on a held-out atlas before it is kept. For {\f1 broad_mouse_v1} the test set is carved from the census itself: two datasets are excluded from the reference sample and re-pulled as the query, so \u8220"held out\u8221" means held-out {\i datasets} and not merely held-out cells.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Distilling a pretrained annotator\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A broad reference can also be built without labels and without a GPU, by taking both the label vocabulary and the hierarchy from an existing pretrained annotator (S6). Keras/TensorFlow and JAX cannot share a process, so this runs in two stages.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Teacher pass.} Pan-human Azimuth labels a corpus of raw counts through its low-level API in batches of 8,192, which loads the weights once rather than once per call. All eight levels are retained. Where the package cannot reconcile a refined level with its coarser ones it blanks that column; those cells still carry a deepest-level call, so we fall back to it rather than discard them \u8212- dropping them would distill only the cells the teacher found easy. Predictions are mapped to CL ids through the package\u8217's own crosswalk.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Student pass.} The student is a hierarchical actinn-jax reference whose {\b classes} are the teacher\u8217's refined fine labels (including its trained {\f1 Unassigned} quality-control class) and whose {\b hierarchy} is the teacher\u8217's own broad level \u8212- so the coarse\u8594 ?fine structure is inherited rather than rediscovered. 4,000-gene HVG panel; classes with fewer than 8 cells dropped, since a class that cannot be split into train and test makes fidelity depend on which side its cells landed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Corpus.} Three local atlases (lung, liver, blood+gut) capped at 600 cells per label, plus a census-wide pull (\u8804 ?60 per type). The atlases\u8217' own labels are used {\b only for scoring}, never for training.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Evaluation.} Two arms answer different questions. {\i In-corpus} holds out a stratified 25% of cells and measures {\b fidelity} \u8212- how often the student reproduces the teacher, exactly and ontology-equivalently. {\i Held-out atlas} withholds an entire atlas and measures whether the student generalizes to data the corpus never covered. Both arms also score student and teacher against the atlases\u8217' own labels, which separates distillation loss from teacher error. Note that once the census pull is in the corpus, a withheld {\i atlas} is no longer a withheld {\i tissue}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Refinement, abstention, and novelty\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Four mechanisms operate on a trained reference at inference time, all label-free.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b Confidence abstention.} Cells whose final probability is below a threshold are relabeled \u8220"unknown\u8221" rather than forced to the nearest class. Calibrated per reference (\u167 ?2.6).\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b Refinement to the query.} The reference\u8217's class set is masked to those classes the query\u8217's own predictions actually support, and the softmax renormalized \u8212- no ground truth, no retraining.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b Refinement to a tissue.} Classes are masked to those the census records in the given tissue, while {\b pan-tissue} types (immune, endothelial, stromal) stay available everywhere, so liver-resident T cells are unaffected. The per-class tissue map is baked into the shipped reference; the tissue is given explicitly or read from the query\u8217's own metadata, with common synonyms (PBMC\u8594 ?blood, hepatic\u8594 ?liver) recognized. A tissue outside the reference\u8217's vocabulary imposes no filter rather than an empty one.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b Cluster-level novelty screen.} A cell type absent from the reference appears not as scattered uncertain cells but as a {\i coherent group the reference cannot confidently explain}. Clusters that are both large enough to be a real population and predominantly low-confidence are flagged, with marker genes reported for each. This is distinct from per-cell abstention.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Within-type state.} The same machinery resolves structure below a cell-type label: a hepatocyte zonation model trained on portal\u8594 ?central zone labels, scored by within-one-zone agreement across donors and datasets (S7).\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Scaling protocol\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The reference is subsampled to five sizes up to {\b 49k cells} with the query held fixed, so that accuracy, fit time, predict time and peak memory are functions of reference size alone. Reported per method. The sweep exists to find where one method overtakes another as the reference grows, which no single reference size can show (S3).\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 External validation protocol\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Open Problems {\f1 label_projection}} {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://openproblems.bio"}}{\fldrslt{\ul
[Open Problems 2024]
}}}
 (S8) supplies the datasets, metrics and ranking \u8212- none chosen by us. actinn-jax is packaged as a viash 0.9.7 component declaring the normalization it expects, and run through the project\u8217's own Nextflow workflow. We additionally built components for four baselines (linear-anova-pca, scTOP, SVM, CellTypist) so the same-hardware comparison covers more than the leaderboard\u8217's own set.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Where each number is measured.} The paper reports two environments. The in-house panel (\u167 ?3.1\u8211-\u167 ?3.5) runs on the Apple Silicon laptop described in \u167 ?2.5. The Open Problems results (\u167 ?3.7) run on AWS {\f1 r7i.8xlarge} instances (32 vCPU, 247 GB) through OP\u8217's own Nextflow pipeline, two tasks at a time.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Accuracy} in Table 10 comes from a single AWS run covering all eleven methods, so every score is one harness, one machine, one invocation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Cost} is reported as a ratio to actinn-jax rather than in seconds, because wall-clock on a shared box depends on what else is running: measured {\f1 %cpu} spans 49% to 2338% across these methods, and actinn-jax itself averaged 165 s/dataset when scheduled alongside OP\u8217's seven and 87 s/dataset alongside our four. Ratios taken within one run are stable where absolute times are not, and actinn-jax was included in both runs to join them. Peak memory does not move with contention and is reported as measured.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Ablations\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Three cheap interventions characterize {\i when} a gene-space MLP gains or loses against heavier methods, all selectable without test labels:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b Input standardization} \u8212- z-score each selected gene by the reference\u8217's frozen mean/std and apply to the query (a scArches-style domain alignment). Opt-in, because it shifts the probability calibration the abstain thresholds are tuned against.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b Gene budget} \u8212- Open Problems feeds every method 1,000 HVGs; we sweep wider panels and select per dataset by {\b held-out reference cross-validation}, which is label-free with respect to the test set.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b Protein-embedding featurization} (negative control) \u8212- a CPU-only featurization in the style of {\b Universal Cell Embeddings (UCE)} {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://doi.org/10.1038/s41586-026-10689-z"}}{\fldrslt{\ul
[Rosen 2026]
}}}
 \u8212- an expression-weighted mean of ESM2 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://doi.org/10.1126/science.ade2574"}}{\fldrslt{\ul
[Lin 2023]
}}}
 protein-language-model gene embeddings \u8212- to test whether a foundation model\u8217's value survives a cheap pooling shortcut.\sa180\par}
