{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Table 5.} Per-dataset accuracy: actinn-jax against whichever method leads that dataset. \u8224 ? on lung_cross the exact-match score is a vocabulary artifact, so ontology concordance is the meaningful column.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 No single method leads everywhere: the linear pipeline and ProtoCloud each take two datasets, actinn-jax and scArches one apiece. actinn-jax leads the cross-study liver split \u8212- the hardest generalization regime here, and the one closest to real reference mapping \u8212- and is second on within-dataset liver, while trailing on lung, on the 86-type blood+gut set (\u8722 ?4.2 pt to the linear pipeline), and on small-n pbmc. The spread across the leading methods on any one dataset is 1\u8211-4 points. scmap-cluster is consistently the weakest (0.23 macro-F1 on liver_cross).\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b \u8224 ? lung_cross exact accuracy (~0.35 for every method) is a label-vocabulary artifact, not a transfer failure.} HLCA (reference) and Krasnow (query) were annotated independently and share only {\b 20 of 46 cell-type names}; 26 of Krasnow\u8217's types are absent from HLCA\u8217's vocabulary, so no classifier can emit the exact string. The mismatch is granularity: HLCA\u8217's finer taxonomy has {\f1 alveolar / elicited / lung macrophage} but {\b no generic {\f1 macrophage}} (CL:0000235), while Krasnow labels many cells generic {\f1 macrophage} \u8212- so an HLCA-trained model predicts {\f1 lung macrophage} (CL:1001603) for them, exact-wrong but ontology-correct ({\i lung macrophage is-a macrophage}); likewise Krasnow\u8217's generic {\f1 endothelial cell} (CL:0000115) has no HLCA counterpart. {\b Ontology-aware concordance \u8212- which credits a same-lineage call \u8212- is ~0.75 for the trained methods (0.67\u8211-0.79 across the full panel, highest for ProtoCloud)}, so cross-dataset transfer actually works; exact-match just conflates classifier error with vocabulary/granularity mismatch. This is precisely why we report ontology concordance, and it is a general benchmarking pitfall: {\b cross-dataset exact accuracy between independently-annotated atlases is not a meaningful accuracy signal.} The other five datasets use a single shared label vocabulary for reference and query (a split of one atlas, or HLiCA\u8217's harmonized annotations), so their exact scores are unaffected.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 The accuracy \u215 ? speed frontier\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\pict\pngblip\picw895\pich616\picwgoal9205\pichgoal6336 }\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Figure 1.} Accuracy against total wall time on liver_intra. The tuned linear pipeline holds the fast-frontier point; actinn-jax sits just inside it at this reference size, and what separates it is visible only on the scaling axes of Figure 2.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The {\b tuned linear pipeline} holds the fast-frontier point: highest matrix accuracy (0.839) at the lowest fit time of any accurate method (3.0 s), placing it above and to the left of actinn-jax, SVM, kNN and CellTypist. The deep methods buy little or nothing on accuracy here \u8212- at or below the linear pipeline on every dataset except lung \u8212- for 30\u8211-130\u215 ? the inference cost. actinn-jax sits just inside the frontier at this reference size; what separates it is not visible on a fixed-size plot but on the scaling axes of \u167 ?3.3 and \u167 ?5: predict time that stays flat as the reference grows, and ~2\u215 ? lower peak memory that holds to atlas scale. The figure is drawn from the liver_intra panel and depicts the ranking of Tables 3 and 5.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Scaling\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\pict\pngblip\picw1665\pich615\picwgoal17125\pichgoal6325 }\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Figure 2.} Fit and predict time against reference size and label cardinality. Fit time grows for every trained method; predict time stays flat and sub-second across the whole range.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Training time grows with reference size and with #cell types for all trained methods \u8212- actinn-jax\u8217's fit goes 2.6 s \u8594 ? 17.4 s \u8594 ? 23.5 s as the reference grows 965 \u8594 ? 14.8k \u8594 ? 24k cells, below CellTypist (6 s \u8594 ? 71 s \u8594 ? 116 s) and SVM (4 s \u8594 ? 51 s \u8594 ? 80 s) at every size in the sweep; kNN is lazy (fit is trivial but it pays at predict and is the least accurate).\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The other axis is the one that matters for the workflows: {\b predict time stays flat and sub-second across the entire range \u8212- 0.08\u8211-0.33 s whether the reference has 1k or 24k cells, or 5 or 86 types.} Inference cost does not grow with reference size or cardinality, and with the train-once/map-many cache the fit cost is paid once and the flat predict cost is all that recurs \u8212- the regime that matters when a reference is reused across many queries. (The scaling script\u8217's memory column is process-cumulative and not a clean per-size measurement; per-method peak memory is reported from the isolated-subprocess matrix in \u167 ?3.2.)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 The broad\u8594 ?focused annotation workflow\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What actinn-jax is built around is not a single classifier but a {\b two-pass workflow} that matches how annotation is actually done: get a broad call fast, then sharpen it where it matters. Because every stage is a cached {\f1 ReferenceModel} with sub-second, memory-bounded inference (\u167 ?3.1, \u167 ?3.3), the whole workflow runs on a laptop.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\pict\pngblip\picw2677\pich988\picwgoal19371\pichgoal7149 }\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Figure 3.} The workflow on a withheld HLiCA liver study (3,396 cells), same embedding throughout. {\i Left:} the shipped census reference spreads 137 of its 798 labels over the query (concordance {\b 0.34}) \u8212- it is not trying to name subtypes. {\i Centre:} those calls resolved to tissue through the reference\u8217's per-class tissue map; {\b 76%} of tissue-specific calls say liver against 4% for the next candidate, which is the decision that selects the next reference. {\i Right of centre:} the 36-type liver reference re-annotates the same cells at {\b 0.73}, now tracking the clusters. {\i Right:} the study\u8217's own labels on the shared palette. Both models are leakage-free for this query: the focused reference is trained only on the six non-withheld studies. Abstention is off in all panels, so the numbers measure labelling rather than coverage; \u167 ?3.5 covers abstain separately.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b The broad pass \u8212- census-scale.} A shipped ~800-type human reference built from the CELLxGENE census gives any query a first-pass annotation across the whole body, with a {\b calibrated abstain threshold} so out-of-reference cells are flagged rather than force-labeled (S9, and the smooth abstain curve of \u167 ?3.5). This is the \u8220"what am I looking at\u8221" pass \u8212- broad coverage, deliberately not fine-grained.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b The broad pass can be built from a model instead of from labels.} The census route above needs a foundation model on a GPU to discover its hierarchy. A pretrained pan-human annotator already has one \u8212- Pan-human Azimuth publishes an 8-level typology with every node mapped to a Cell Ontology term \u8212- so labelling a corpus with it and training actinn-jax on those labels transfers both the vocabulary and the structure. That build needs {\b no GPU and no labeled input}, only raw human counts: under ten minutes of CPU on 85k cells drawn from three atlases plus a census-wide sample (S6). On 3,396 withheld cross-study liver cells:\par}
